   "_id": 27598,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"542\" data-start=\"369\"><strong data-end=\"395\" data-start=\"369\">Signed binary addition</strong> is used to add both positive and negative numbers in binary form. One common way to represent signed numbers in binary is <strong data-end=\"536\" data-start=\"518\">2&rsquo;s complement</strong> form.</p>\n\n<h4 data-end=\"579\" data-start=\"544\">\ud83e\udde0 <strong data-end=\"579\" data-start=\"552\">What is 2&rsquo;s Complement?</strong></h4>\n\n<p data-end=\"742\" data-start=\"580\">2&rsquo;s complement allows us to represent both positive and negative integers using binary. It simplifies subtraction and makes addition of signed numbers consistent.</p>\n\n<ul data-end=\"958\" data-start=\"744\">\n\t<li data-end=\"802\" data-start=\"744\">\n\t<p data-end=\"802\" data-start=\"746\"><strong data-end=\"766\" data-start=\"746\">Positive numbers</strong> are represented normally in binary.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"958\" data-start=\"803\">\n\t<p data-end=\"901\" data-start=\"805\"><strong data-end=\"825\" data-start=\"805\">Negative numbers</strong> are represented by taking the 2&rsquo;s complement of the positive binary number:</p>\n\n\t<ul data-end=\"958\" data-start=\"904\">\n\t\t<li data-end=\"934\" data-start=\"904\">\n\t\t<p data-end=\"934\" data-start=\"906\">1&rsquo;s complement (invert bits)</p>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li data-end=\"958\" data-start=\"937\">\n\t\t<p data-end=\"958\" data-start=\"939\">Add 1 to the result</p>\n\t\t</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p data-end=\"496\" data-start=\"387\"><strong data-end=\"428\" data-start=\"387\">Binary of +78 (8-bit 2&rsquo;s complement):</strong> <code data-end=\"439\" data-start=\"429\">01001110</code><br data-end=\"442\" data-start=\"439\" />\n<strong data-end=\"483\" data-start=\"442\">Binary of &ndash;45 (8-bit 2&rsquo;s complement):</strong> <code data-end=\"494\" data-start=\"484\">11010011</code></p>\n\n<p data-end=\"577\" data-start=\"498\"><strong data-end=\"533\" data-start=\"498\">Binary Addition Result (8-bit):</strong> <code data-end=\"544\" data-start=\"534\">00100001</code><br data-end=\"547\" data-start=\"544\" />\n<strong data-end=\"570\" data-start=\"547\">Decimal Equivalent:</strong> <code data-end=\"575\" data-start=\"571\">33</code></p>\n",
